    "knowledge_point": "Determining Open Intervals of Continuity",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/43786cb577705d6ae13d44e75203fef441905e9ccfc3713726f07d36c147c7b3.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous? You may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram.",
    "worked_solution": "To determine the intervals of continuity of a function, we take the domain of the function and exclude any points of discontinuity. \n\nThe function is defined on the interval $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The function is discontinuous at $x=-3$ and at $x=3,$ so we need to exclude these points..\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-3,3),$ and $(-3,\\infty ).$  It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following: \n\n$\\,$ $(-\\infty ,-3)$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $(-4,-2)$ and $(-\\infty ,1)$ contain the excluded point $-3,$  so the function is not continuous on these intervals.\n\n- $(-2,4)$ and $(2,\\infty )$ contain the excluded point $3,$ so the function is not continuous on these intervals.",
